    slug: "tortillas-flour",
    title: "Flour Tortillas",
    subtitle: "Sonoran-style flour tortillas with lard, rested dough, cooked on a dry comal",
    description: "Northern Mexican flour tortillas: flour, lard, salt, and warm water, rested so the gluten relaxes, then rolled thin and cooked hot and fast on a dry skillet until they puff and freckle. Soft, pliable, with that faint lard sweetness — orders of magnitude better than the supermarket version.",
    yield: { quantity: 12, unit: "tortillas", raw: "12 tortillas (about 20 cm each)" },
    times: { prepMinutes: 20, cookMinutes: 20, totalMinutes: 70 },
    ingredients: [
      { id: "flour", raw: "500 g all-purpose flour, plus more for dusting", quantity: "500", unit: "g", item: "all-purpose flour", section: "Dough" },
      { id: "salt", raw: "1 1/2 tsp fine salt", quantity: "1.5", unit: "tsp", item: "fine salt", section: "Dough" },
      { id: "baking-powder", raw: "1/2 tsp baking powder, optional", quantity: "0.5", unit: "tsp", item: "baking powder", optional: true, section: "Dough" },
      { id: "lard", raw: "80 g lard (manteca), at room temperature", quantity: "80", unit: "g", item: "lard, at room temperature", section: "Dough" },
      { id: "water", raw: "280 ml warm water (about 40 C)", quantity: "280", unit: "ml", item: "warm water", section: "Dough" },
    ],
    steps: [
      { id: "dry-mix", section: "Dough", text: "Whisk the flour, salt, and baking powder if using in a wide bowl until evenly combined.", ingredientRefs: ["flour", "salt", "baking-powder"] },
      { id: "cut-fat", section: "Dough", text: "Add the lard. Rub it into the flour with your fingertips until the mixture looks like coarse, slightly damp sand — no visible lumps of fat.", ingredientRefs: ["lard"] },
      { id: "hydrate", section: "Dough", text: "Pour in the warm water and stir with a wooden spoon, then your hand, until a shaggy dough comes together. Turn out onto an unfloured counter.", ingredientRefs: ["water"] },
      { id: "knead", section: "Dough", text: "Knead for about 3 minutes until the dough is smooth, soft, and springs back slowly when you press it. It should feel like a relaxed earlobe — supple, not stiff. If it is sticky, add a tablespoon of flour; if dry, a teaspoon of water.", timerSeconds: 180 },
      { id: "portion", section: "Dough", text: "Divide the dough into 12 equal pieces (about 70 g each). Roll each into a tight ball, tucking the seams underneath. Arrange on a tray and cover with a damp kitchen towel." },
      { id: "rest", section: "Dough", text: "Let the dough balls rest at room temperature so the gluten relaxes — this is what makes the tortillas roll thin without springing back.", timerSeconds: 1800 },
      { id: "heat-comal", section: "Cooking", text: "Set a dry cast-iron skillet or comal over medium-high heat until a drop of water dances and evaporates in a second. No oil; the surface stays bone dry.", temperature: { value: 230, unit: "c", raw: "medium-high (about 230 C surface)" } },
      { id: "roll", section: "Cooking", text: "On a lightly floured surface, roll a dough ball with a rolling pin into a thin round about 20 cm across — thin enough to almost see the counter through. Rotate a quarter turn between strokes to keep it round." },
      { id: "cook", section: "Cooking", text: "Lay the rolled tortilla onto the dry comal. Cook for about 30 seconds until the underside is freckled and small bubbles rise on top. Flip and cook another 30-45 seconds — the tortilla should puff in patches as the steam expands.", timerSeconds: 75 },
      { id: "stack", section: "Cooking", text: "Move each cooked tortilla onto a clean kitchen towel and wrap to keep warm and steam-soften. Roll the next while the current one cooks — it is a rhythm." },
      { id: "serve", section: "Cooking", text: "Serve warm, straight from the towel. Best within an hour of cooking." },
    ],
    notes: [
      "Lard is the right fat. Pork lard (manteca) gives Sonoran tortillas their characteristic sweetness and tenderness. Vegetable shortening is the acceptable substitute and gives a neutral, slightly less tender tortilla. Butter belongs in biryani, not flour tortillas — its water content makes them tough and the dairy note is wrong.",
      "Common mistake — rolling cold dough. If the dough has not rested, it will spring back the moment you lift the pin and you will end up with thick, doughy tortillas. The rest is non-negotiable; 30 minutes is the minimum, an hour is better.",
      "Variation — thicker northern style. Larger Sonoran tortillas (40 cm, paper-thin) require stretching by hand on a cloth. For everyday burrito-size tortillas, the 20 cm rolled version here is the right scale.",
      "Storage. Best within an hour of cooking. Cooled tortillas keep 3 days in a zip-top bag in the fridge or 1 month in the freezer; reheat 10 seconds per side on a hot dry skillet — never microwave from cold, they go rubbery.",
      "Serving guidance. Wrap around carnitas, scrambled eggs and chorizo, grilled steak, or beans and cheese. A warm tortilla with a smear of butter and salt is also a perfectly respectable snack.",
    ],
    tags: ["baseline", "baking", "mexican", "bread", "vegetarian"],
    collections: ["Baseline", "Baking", "Bread", "Mexican"],
